WEBVTT
TITLE Counting Spanning Trees — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A spanning tree keeps every vertex and exactly n-1 edges, with no cycle.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
K4 has six edges, so there are C(6,3) = 20 ways to choose three of them.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Four of those 20 choices are triangles, leaving one vertex out: 20 - 4 = 16.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Sixteen is the count for K4, and it is what the 2024 paper asked for.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Brute force: choose n-1 of the m edges, then test that the choice is connected.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
C4 has four edges, and any three of them form a path: 4 trees, one per omitted edge.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
K2,3 has five vertices, so a tree needs four edges: 15 subsets, and 12 of them are trees.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Choose-then-test is exact but costs C(m, n-1) checks, so it is only for small graphs.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Deletion-contraction splits on one edge: trees that avoid it, plus trees that use it.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Avoiding e gives G-e; using e gives G/e, where the two ends become one vertex.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
On the diamond the two branches give 4 and 4, and 4 + 4 = 8.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The recursion stops at two base cases: one vertex counts 1, no edges on two or more counts 0.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
On a complete graph the count collapses to a formula: n to the power n-2.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
For n = 1 to 7 the counts are 1, 1, 3, 16, 125, 1296, 16807.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The exponent is n-2 because a tree is pinned down by n-2 choices, not n-1.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Cayley needs a complete graph. On a sparse graph the formula over-counts.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A Prüfer code is what you get by deleting the smallest leaf, over and over.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Record the neighbour of each deleted leaf, and stop when two vertices are left.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
This six-vertex tree gives the code 1, 2, 4, 4 — four labels, which is n-2.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Every tree on n labelled vertices gives exactly one code, so no tree is missed.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
To reverse it, count each label in the code and add one: that is its degree.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The smallest label of degree one must be a leaf: join it to the first code entry.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Repeat, then join the last two survivors: the tree comes back exactly.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
So codes and trees match one for one: 5 to the 3 is 125 trees on five vertices.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The Laplacian is the degree on the diagonal, minus one for each edge.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Delete any one row and the same column; the determinant is the tree count.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
For K4 the minor has 3 on the diagonal and -1 elsewhere: determinant 16.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
For C5 the same recipe gives 5, and for K3,3 it gives 81.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A tree counts 1, and a disconnected graph counts 0.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A cycle C_n counts n: one tree for each edge you delete.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
A complete bipartite graph K_m,n counts m to the n-1 times n to the m-1.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
The wheel W4 counts 45, and the determinant gives it without a formula.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Labelled or unlabelled decides which number the question wants.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
On four vertices there are 16 labelled trees but only 2 shapes: the path and the star.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Binary search trees on four keys number 14, the fourth Catalan number.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So 16, 2 and 14 answer three different questions about the same four vertices.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: is the graph complete, a cycle, or neither? That picks the method.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: check connectivity first. A disconnected graph has no spanning tree.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: on K4, brute force, contraction and the determinant all give 16.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The 2024 paper asked exactly this for K4 labelled, and the official key is 16.

